
.TH LOGNAME 1 "October 2026" "logname" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
logname \- print the login username
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logname
.RB [ \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logname
prints the username associated with the current login session.
.PP
Unlike
.BR whoami ,
which reports the effective username,
.B logname
reports the user who originally logged into the session,
even if the effective user ID has changed through
.B sudo
or
.BR su .
.PP
The login name is obtained through the system's
.BR getlogin (3)
function, which typically uses the controlling terminal
and login session records.
.PP
If no login session can be identified,
.B logname
prints an error message and exits with a nonzero status.
This may occur in containers, scheduled jobs, or processes
without a controlling terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display the login username:
.PP
.RS
.B logname
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B user
.RE
.PP
Display the login username after switching to another user:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sudo -i
# whoami
root
# logname
user
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or argument was specified,
the login name could not be determined,
or an output error occurred.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR logname .
The
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whoami (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR users (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR getlogin (3)
